h3 2.1.2 Resources
text The specification uses the term  when referring to whether a user agent has an implementation capable of decoding the semantics of an external resource. A format or type is said to be  if the implementation can process an external resource of that format or type without critical aspects of the resource being ignored. Whether a specific resource is  can depend on what features of the resource's format are in use.
text supported
text supported
text supported
text For example, a PNG image would be considered to be in a supported format if its pixel data could be decoded and rendered, even if, unbeknownst to the implementation, the image also contained animation data.
text An MPEG-4 video file would not be considered to be in a supported format if the compression format used was not supported, even if the implementation could determine the dimensions of the movie from the file's metadata.
text What some specifications, in particular the HTTP specifications, refer to as a  is referred to in this specification as a .
row
  text representation
  text resource
  link [HTTP] references.html#refsHTTP
text A resource's  are those that the resource needs to have available to be correctly processed. Which resources are considered critical or not is defined by the specification that defines the resource's format.
text critical subresources
text For , we tentatively define here that their critical subresources are other style sheets imported via  rules, including those indirectly imported by other imported style sheets.
row
  link "CSS style sheets" https://drafts.csswg.org/cssom/#css-style-sheet
