h3 2.1.6 Plugins
text The term  refers to an  set of content handlers used by the user agent that can take part in the user agent's rendering of a  object, but that neither act as  of the  nor introduce any  objects to the 's DOM.
text plugin
link implementation-defined https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#implementation-defined
link Document dom.html#document
link "child navigables" document-sequences.html#child-navigable
link Document dom.html#document
link Node https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#interface-node
link Document dom.html#document
text Typically such content handlers are provided by third parties, though a user agent can also designate built-in content handlers as plugins.
text A user agent must not consider the types  and  as having a registered .
row
  link text/plain https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2046#section-4.1.3
  link application/octet-stream https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2046#section-4.5.1
  link plugin #plugin
text One example of a plugin would be a PDF viewer that is instantiated in a  when the user navigates to a PDF file. This would count as a plugin regardless of whether the party that implemented the PDF viewer component was the same as that which implemented the user agent itself. However, a PDF viewer application that launches separate from the user agent (as opposed to using the same interface) is not a plugin by this definition.
link navigable document-sequences.html#navigable
text This specification does not define a mechanism for interacting with plugins, as it is expected to be user-agent- and platform-specific. Some UAs might opt to support a plugin mechanism such as the Netscape Plugin API; others might use remote content converters or have built-in support for certain types. Indeed, this specification doesn't require user agents to support plugins at all. success
link [NPAPI] references.html#refsNPAPI
text Browsers should take extreme care when interacting with external content intended for . When third-party software is run with the same privileges as the user agent itself, vulnerabilities in the third-party software become as dangerous as those in the user agent. danger
link plugins #plugin
text Since different users having different sets of  provides a tracking vector that increases the chances of users being uniquely identified, user agents are encouraged to support the exact same set of  for each user.
