h3 2.1.8 Conformance classes
text This specification describes the conformance criteria for user agents (relevant to implementers) and documents (relevant to authors and authoring tool implementers).
text are those that comply with all the conformance criteria for documents. For readability, some of these conformance requirements are phrased as conformance requirements on authors; such requirements are implicitly requirements on documents: by definition, all documents are assumed to have had an author. (In some cases, that author may itself be a user agent — such user agents are subject to additional rules, as explained below.)
text Conforming documents
text "For example, if a requirement states that \"authors must not use the foobar element\", it would imply that documents are not allowed to contain elements named foobar."
text There is no implied relationship between document conformance requirements and implementation conformance requirements. User agents are not free to handle non-conformant documents as they please; the processing model described in this specification applies to implementations regardless of the conformity of the input documents. success
text User agents fall into several (overlapping) categories with different conformance requirements.
text Web browsers and other interactive user agents
text Web browsers that support  must process elements and attributes from the  found in XML documents as described in this specification, so that users can interact with them, unless the semantics of those elements have been overridden by other specifications.
row
  link "the XML syntax" xhtml.html#the-xhtml-syntax
  link "HTML namespace" https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#html-namespace
text A conforming web browser would, upon finding a  element in an XML document, execute the script contained in that element. However, if the element is found within a transformation expressed in XSLT (assuming the user agent also supports XSLT), then the processor would instead treat the  element as an opaque element that forms part of the transform.
link script scripting.html#the-script-element
link script scripting.html#the-script-element
text Web browsers that support  must process documents labeled with an  as described in this specification, so that users can interact with them.
